WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:06.990
Of all the effects of the economic crisis
in Europe, the one real scourge is youth unemployment.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:13.160
Some 5.6 million young people aged between
15 and 24 were jobless in the European Union

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.350
in September.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.520
The unemployment rate for them is more than
twice that for adults.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.050
In Croatia, Greece and Spain, half of young
people are out of work.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.320
That's why European leaders have been taking
extraordinary steps to address this urgent

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.660
problem, as demonstrated by the recent Paris
Conference on Youth Employment.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.480
Four main measures are being taken to get
young people out of Europe's jobless queues.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:43.100
The first is the youth guarantee scheme, aimed
at ensuring that all people under 25 receive

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.850
a quality offer of employment, further education
or a traineeship within four months of leaving

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:49.890
school.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:55.739
Second, the youth employment initiative allows
funding from a 6 million euro total budget

00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:00.600
for young people in regions where jobless
rates are more than 25 percent.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.210
Thirdly, quality traineeships aimed at easing
the transition from education to full-time

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:12.190
employment, and also an effort to change public
attitudes towards apprenticeship-style learning.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.869
Finally, encouraging young people to look
for work across Europe's borders in neighbouring

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:22.510
states, through labour mobility programmes
like the Eures job initiative.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:28.549
The portal links users to more than 1.4 million
job vacancies and over 300,000 registered

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:30.219
employers.

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:35.789
These measures are all part of Europe's comprehensive
programme to tackle this number one priority.

